# React & TypeScript Patterns

## Component Structure

### File Organization

```
components/
  FeatureName/
    FeatureName.tsx          # Main component
    FeatureName.spec.tsx     # Tests
    index.ts                 # Exports
    types.ts                 # TypeScript interfaces (if needed)

  ui/
    SharedComponent/         # Reusable UI components (each in own folder)
      SharedComponent.tsx
      SharedComponent.spec.tsx
      index.ts
```

### UI Folder Components - Reusability First

**Always check the `ui/` folder for existing components before creating new ones. Consider adding new components to `ui/` if they could be reused.**

**Before creating a new component, ask:**

1. Does a similar component already exist in `src/components/ui/`?
2. Could this component be used in multiple places?
3. Is this a generic UI pattern (buttons, inputs, cards, modals)?

```typescript
// ✅ Good: Use existing UI components
import { GenericCard, EditableInputForm } from '@/components/ui'

const UserProfile = () => {
  return (
    <GenericCard>
      <EditableInputForm label="Name" name="name" control={control} />
    </GenericCard>
  )
}

// ✅ Good: Create new UI component in its own folder for shared use
// src/components/ui/StatusBadge/StatusBadge.tsx
export const StatusBadge = ({ status, label }) => {
  return (
    <Badge colorScheme={getStatusColor(status)}>
      {label}
    </Badge>
  )
}

// src/components/ui/StatusBadge/index.ts
export { StatusBadge } from './StatusBadge'
export type { StatusBadgeProps } from './StatusBadge'

// ❌ Bad: Creating similar components in multiple feature folders
// Don't duplicate UI patterns - extract to components/ui/ folder instead
```

**When to add to components/ui/ folder:**

- Generic UI patterns (buttons, inputs, cards, badges)
- Components used in 2+ different features
- Design system components
- Layout components (modals, drawers, containers)

**When to keep in feature folder:**

- Feature-specific business logic
- Components tightly coupled to a single feature
- Complex domain-specific components

### Component Patterns

```typescript
// ✅ Good: Named export with interface
interface UserCardProps {
  user: User
  onEdit?: (user: User) => void
}

export const UserCard = ({ user, onEdit }: UserCardProps) => {
  return (
    <div className="user-card">
      <h3>{user.name}</h3>
      {onEdit && (
        <button onClick={() => onEdit(user)}>
          Edit
        </button>
      )}
    </div>
  )
}
```

### Component Architecture & Separation of Concerns

#### Keep Components Clean and Focused

**❌ Bad: Large component with mixed responsibilities**

```typescript
const CasesView: React.FC = () => {
  const [currentPage, setCurrentPage] = useState(1)
  const [searchTerm, setSearchTerm] = useState('')

  // Business logic mixed with UI
  const handlePageChange = (page: number) => setCurrentPage(page)
  const formatDate = (dateString: string) => new Date(dateString).toLocaleDateString()

  // Complex pagination calculations
  const totalPages = Math.ceil(totalItems / itemsPerPage)
  const offset = (currentPage - 1) * itemsPerPage

  return (
    <Box>
      {/* Mixed UI concerns */}
      <Input placeholder="Search..." value={searchTerm} onChange={handleSearch} />
      <VStack>
        {cases.map(case => (
          <Card key={case.id}>
            <Text>{case.name}</Text>
            <Text>{formatDate(case.createdAt)}</Text>
          </Card>
        ))}
      </VStack>
      {/* Inline pagination controls */}
      <HStack>
        <Button onClick={() => handlePageChange(currentPage - 1)}>Prev</Button>
        {Array.from({ length: totalPages }, (_, i) => (
          <Button key={i} onClick={() => handlePageChange(i + 1)}>{i + 1}</Button>
        ))}
        <Button onClick={() => handlePageChange(currentPage + 1)}>Next</Button>
      </HStack>
    </Box>
  )
}
```

**✅ Good: Separated concerns with hooks and reusable components**

```typescript
// Custom hook for pagination logic
const usePagination = (totalItems: number, itemsPerPage: number) => {
  const [currentPage, setCurrentPage] = useState(1)
  const totalPages = Math.ceil(totalItems / itemsPerPage)
  const offset = (currentPage - 1) * itemsPerPage

  return {
    currentPage,
    totalPages,
    offset,
    goToPage: (page: number) => setCurrentPage(page),
    goToNextPage: () => setCurrentPage(prev => prev + 1),
    goToPrevPage: () => setCurrentPage(prev => prev - 1),
  }
}

// Reusable pagination component
const PaginationControls: React.FC<PaginationControlsProps> = ({
  currentPage,
  totalPages,
  onPageChange,
  onPrevPage,
  onNextPage,
}) => {
  return (
    <HStack>
      <Button onClick={onPrevPage}>Previous</Button>
      {Array.from({ length: Math.min(5, totalPages) }, (_, i) => {
        const pageNum = Math.max(1, currentPage - 2) + i
        return (
          <Button key={pageNum} onClick={() => onPageChange(pageNum)}>
            {pageNum}
          </Button>
        )
      })}
      <Button onClick={onNextPage}>Next</Button>
    </HStack>
  )
}

// Clean, focused component
const CasesView: React.FC = () => {
  const pagination = usePagination(totalItems, ITEMS_PER_PAGE)
  const { data: cases } = useCasesQuery({
    limit: ITEMS_PER_PAGE,
    offset: pagination.offset,
  })

  return (
    <VStack spacing={6}>
      <VStack spacing={4}>
        {cases?.map(case => (
          <GenericCard key={case.id}>
            <Text>{case.name}</Text>
            <Text>{formatDate(case.createdAt)}</Text>
          </GenericCard>
        ))}
      </VStack>

      <PaginationControls
        currentPage={pagination.currentPage}
        totalPages={pagination.totalPages}
        onPageChange={pagination.goToPage}
        onPrevPage={pagination.goToPrevPage}
        onNextPage={pagination.goToNextPage}
      />
    </VStack>
  )
}
```

#### Best Practices for Component Separation

**🎯 Single Responsibility Principle**

- Each component should have one clear purpose
- Extract complex logic into custom hooks
- Create reusable UI components for repeated patterns

**🔧 Custom Hooks for Logic**

```typescript
// ✅ Extract business logic to hooks
const usePagination = (totalItems: number, itemsPerPage: number) => {
  // Logic here
}

const useSearch = (items: Item[]) => {
  const [searchTerm, setSearchTerm] = useState('')
  const filteredItems = useMemo(() => {
    return items.filter((item) =>
      item.name.toLowerCase().includes(searchTerm.toLowerCase())
    )
  }, [items, searchTerm])

  return { searchTerm, setSearchTerm, filteredItems }
}
```

**🧩 Reusable UI Components**

```typescript
// ✅ Create reusable components
const PaginationControls = ({ currentPage, totalPages, onPageChange }) => {
  // UI logic here
}

const DataTable = ({ data, columns, onSort }) => {
  // Table logic here
}

const SearchInput = ({ value, onChange, placeholder }) => {
  // Search UI here
}
```

**📁 File Organization for Separation**

```
components/
  Cases/
    CasesView.tsx          # Main component (UI orchestration)
    CasesList.tsx          # Cases display logic
    CaseCard.tsx           # Individual case component
    index.ts              # Clean exports

  ui/
    PaginationControls/    # Reusable pagination (in own folder)
      PaginationControls.tsx
      index.ts
    SearchInput/          # Reusable search (in own folder)
      SearchInput.tsx
      index.ts
    DataTable/            # Reusable table (in own folder)
      DataTable.tsx
      index.ts

hooks/
  usePagination.ts       # Pagination logic
  useSearch.ts          # Search logic
  useCasesQuery.ts      # Data fetching
```

#### When to Extract Components

**Extract when:**

- Component has > 200 lines
- Component has multiple responsibilities
- Logic is reused across components
- Component has complex state management
- Component handles multiple data transformations

**Keep together when:**

- Component is small and focused (< 100 lines)
- Logic is unique to that component
- Component is a simple wrapper/presenter

## State Management

### React Query for Server State

```typescript
// ✅ Good: Use React Query for API data
const useUsersQuery = () => {
  return useQuery({
    queryKey: ['users'],
    queryFn: () => HttpClient.getUsers(),
    staleTime: 5 * 60 * 1000, // 5 minutes
  })
}
```

### Local State

```typescript
// ✅ Good: useState for local UI state
const [isEditing, setIsEditing] = useState(false)
const [formData, setFormData] = useState(initialData)
```

## TypeScript Best Practices

### Derive Types from Backend Types - Critical Pattern

**Almost all our types are derived from the backend.** Instead of redefining primitive types, always use `Pick` or `Omit` to derive component props from backend types.

#### ✅ Correct: Derive from Backend Types

```typescript
// Backend type (from src/network/types.ts)
interface Case {
  id: string
  name: string
  user_case_id: string | null
  created_at: string
  updated_at: string
  status: string
}

// ✅ Good: Use Pick to derive component props
interface CaseSettingsContentProps {
  caseData: Pick<Case, 'id' | 'name' | 'user_case_id'>
}

export const CaseSettingsContent: React.FC<CaseSettingsContentProps> = ({
  caseData,
}) => {
  // caseData.id, caseData.name, caseData.user_case_id are all properly typed
  return <SettingsForm caseData={caseData} />
}

// ✅ Good: Use Omit to exclude fields
interface CaseListItemProps {
  case: Omit<Case, 'created_at' | 'updated_at'>
}

// ✅ Good: Extend backend types when adding UI-specific props
interface CaseCardProps {
  case: Pick<Case, 'id' | 'name' | 'status'>
  onEdit?: (id: string) => void
  isSelected?: boolean
}
```

#### ❌ Incorrect: Redefining Primitive Types

```typescript
// ❌ BAD: Manually redefining types that already exist in backend types
interface CaseSettingsContentProps {
  caseId: string // ❌ Don't redefine - use Pick<Case, 'id'>
  caseName: string // ❌ Don't redefine - use Pick<Case, 'name'>
  docketNumber: string | null // ❌ Don't redefine - use Pick<Case, 'user_case_id'>
}

// ❌ BAD: Duplicating entire backend type structure
interface CaseCardProps {
  id: string // ❌ Duplicates Case.id
  name: string // ❌ Duplicates Case.name
  status: string // ❌ Duplicates Case.status
}
```

### Interface Definitions

```typescript
// ✅ Good: Define interfaces for props and data with descriptive generic names
interface ApiResponse<ResponseData> {
  data: ResponseData
  pagination?: Pagination
  error?: string
}

interface User {
  id: string
  name: string
  email: string
  createdAt: string
}
```

### Generic Type Parameter Naming

**Always use descriptive names for generic type parameters instead of single letters.**

Single-letter generics like `T`, `K`, `V` make code harder to read and understand. Use descriptive names that explain what the generic represents.

#### ✅ Correct: Descriptive Generic Names

```typescript
// ✅ Good: Clear what the generic represents
interface ApiResponse<ResponseData> {
  data: ResponseData
  pagination?: Pagination
  error?: string
}

// ✅ Good: Descriptive mapped type key
export type FormData = Required<{
  [FieldKey in keyof CollectionEditRequest]: NonNullable<
    CollectionEditRequest[FieldKey]
  >
}>

// ✅ Good: Multiple generics with clear names
interface Dictionary<KeyType, ValueType> {
  get(key: KeyType): ValueType | undefined
  set(key: KeyType, value: ValueType): void
}

// ✅ Good: Component with descriptive generic
interface SelectProps<OptionValue> {
  options: Array<{ value: OptionValue; label: string }>
  onChange: (value: OptionValue) => void
}
```

#### ❌ Incorrect: Single-Letter Generics

```typescript
// ❌ BAD: What does T represent?
interface ApiResponse<T> {
  data: T
  pagination?: Pagination
  error?: string
}

// ❌ BAD: What does K mean?
export type FormData = Required<{
  [K in keyof CollectionEditRequest]: NonNullable<CollectionEditRequest[K]>
}>

// ❌ BAD: K and V are cryptic
interface Dictionary<K, V> {
  get(key: K): V | undefined
  set(key: K, value: V): void
}
```

#### Common Naming Patterns

- `ResponseData`, `RequestData`, `PayloadData` - for API data types
- `FieldKey`, `PropertyKey`, `RecordKey` - for object keys in mapped types
- `ItemType`, `ElementType` - for array/collection elements
- `OptionValue`, `SelectValue` - for select/dropdown values
- `EntityType`, `ModelType` - for domain models

### Generic Components

```typescript
// ✅ Good: Generic components with proper constraints and descriptive names
interface SelectProps<OptionType> {
  options: OptionType[]
  value: OptionType | null
  onChange: (value: OptionType) => void
  getLabel: (item: OptionType) => string
}

export const Select = <OptionType extends { id: string }>({
  options,
  value,
  onChange,
  getLabel,
}: SelectProps<OptionType>) => {
  // Implementation
}
```

### TypeScript Generics in JSX - Build Tool Compatibility

**Important: Avoid unnecessary generic syntax in JSX to prevent build tool conflicts.**

#### The Problem: Build Tool Conflicts

Some build tools and plugins (like `lovable-tagger`) can conflict with TypeScript generic syntax in JSX:

```typescript
// ❌ Problematic: Can cause build errors with certain plugins
<MultiSelect<string>
  options={options}
  selectedValues={selected}
  onChange={onChange}
/>
```

#### The Solution: Use Default Generic Types

**Always prefer default generic types over explicit generic syntax when possible:**

```typescript
// ✅ Good: Component definition with default generic type
export const MultiSelect = <T extends string | number = string>({
  options,
  selectedValues,
  onChange,
  // ... other props
}: MultiSelectProps<T>) => {
  // Implementation
}

// ✅ Good: Usage without generic syntax (uses default 'string' type)
<MultiSelect
  options={options}
  selectedValues={selected}
  onChange={onChange}
/>

// ❌ Unnecessary: Don't specify generic if it's the default
<MultiSelect<string>  // 'string' is already the default
  options={options}
  selectedValues={selected}
  onChange={onChange}
/>
```

#### When Generic Syntax is Needed

Only use explicit generic syntax when you need a type other than the default:

```typescript
// ✅ Good: Explicit generic needed for non-default type
<MultiSelect<number>
  options={numberOptions}
  selectedValues={selectedNumbers}
  onChange={handleNumberChange}
/>

// ✅ Good: Custom type requires explicit generic
interface CustomOption {
  id: string
  label: string
}

<MultiSelect<CustomOption>
  options={customOptions}
  selectedValues={selectedCustom}
  onChange={handleCustomChange}
/>
```

#### Key Lessons

1. **Question the necessity**: Always ask "Do I really need explicit generic syntax here?"
2. **Default types are powerful**: Well-designed default generic types eliminate most explicit usage
3. **KISS principle**: Simpler code is better code - avoid over-engineering
4. **Build tool compatibility**: Consider how build tools might interpret your TypeScript syntax
5. **Type safety preserved**: Default generics still provide full type checking and IntelliSense

## Performance Considerations

### Memoization

```typescript
// ✅ Good: Memoize expensive calculations
const expensiveValue = useMemo(() => {
  return heavyCalculation(props.data)
}, [props.data])

// ✅ Good: Memoize callbacks
const handleClick = useCallback(() => {
  doSomething(props.id)
}, [props.id])

// ✅ Good: Memoize components when appropriate
const MemoizedComponent = memo(({ data }) => {
  return <div>{data.value}</div>
})
```

